[{"expectations":"Check every card as an expert in the subject. The option marked correct is right, no other option is also right or partly right, and the reveal agrees with it and is true in every detail. Nothing from the never-write list: personal content, records or superlatives that can change, exact figures that aren't standard textbook numbers, anything that changes with time (current laws, rules, taxes, prices, versions, news, an exam's own dates, fees, question counts, scoring or cutoffs), quotes or who said something, \"scientists recently found\", health, legal or money advice (what someone should do), famous myths stated as facts, disputed trivia, jokes at someone's expense. Each card that is false, has a second right option, or breaks the never-write list is its own observation. A checker drops doubtful cards before learners see them, so how many cards fail matters more than how badly.","id":"accuracy","label":"Accuracy and safe topics"},{"expectations":"It works as a pretest for a beginner: each card is about an idea the learner meets in the first weeks of this goal, with a gap the learner wants closed and a surprising but fair answer. The strongest hook comes first, easy before hard, a mix of shapes (how many, which one, which is bigger, finish the sentence), and never two cards on one idea or one card giving away another's answer. Wrong options are ones a beginner might pick and an expert never would. A broad subject gets about 10 cards; a niche or sensitive one may have fewer, and fewer true cards beat a full deck with a doubtful one. Topics a case's expectations list are examples of what fits, not a checklist: leaving one out is never a flaw. Cards that are trivia unrelated to what a beginner will learn, answers obvious from the question, and two cards testing the same idea waste the deck's few cards.","id":"pretest","label":"Pretest value and deck order"},{"expectations":"The question is one sentence of about 50 to 110 characters (it may end in \"…\" to be completed). Options are 1 to 6 words, 2 for \"which is bigger, older, longer\" and 3 otherwise, and none is \"both\", \"all of the above\" or \"none of these\". The reveal is 1 or 2 sentences aiming for 220 characters or fewer, starts with the answer, then gives the why, never repeats the question, and never says whether the learner guessed right. Several cards that break these limits make the deck harder to read at a glance.","id":"format","label":"Card format"},{"expectations":"Written entirely in the learner's language and its variety (Brazilian Portuguese for pt, US English for en, Mexican Spanish for es), natural and correct. A language goal's target-language words are in quotes, exactly as written in that language. The tone is a good YouTube teacher's: everyday words, concrete pictures, a little playful, never silly. No \"Did you know\", \"Fun fact\", exclamation marks or emoji. Cards that mix languages outside quoted target-language words lose the learner, and a tone that's dry like an exam or childish loses the beginner the deck is meant to hook.","id":"language","label":"Language and tone"}]